Production ML Serving & Observability Stack
This repository/task manages a production-ready machine learning fraud-detection platform deployed via Docker Compose. It integrates a Flask API model service, Redis rate limiting, Nginx reverse proxy, Prometheus metrics scraping, and Grafana visualization.

Architecture Overview
Plaintext
 [Traffic Generator] ──> [Nginx Reverse Proxy (:8085)] ──> [Flask Model API (:5000)]
                                                                    │
                                                            [Prometheus (:9090)]
                                                                    │
                                                             [Grafana (:3000)]
model-api: Python 3.11 Flask service loading model.pkl to score transactions via /predict, perform liveness checks at /health, and expose metrics at /metrics using prometheus_flask_exporter.

prod-redis: Redis backend handling per-IP request rate limiting (100 requests / minute) for /predict.

prod-nginx: Reverse proxy routing external traffic from port 8085 to the internal API backend.

prod-prometheus: Time-series database scraping service metrics from model-api:5000.

prod-grafana: Visualization dashboard platform pre-provisioned with the Prometheus datasource and featuring a custom request-rate dashboard.

prod-traffic: Sidecar service continuously sending test POST requests to keep live metrics flowing.

Resolved Wiring Faults
Port Mismatch: Corrected prometheus.yml and nginx.conf where scrape targets and upstream blocks were incorrectly pointed to port 8000, updating them to match the actual Flask container port 5000.

Prometheus Metrics Exporter: Ensured prometheus_flask_exporter (PrometheusMetrics(app)) was correctly initialized inside app/app.py to register the /metrics endpoint.

Grafana Dashboard Provisioning: Programmatically pushed a dashboard via the Grafana API carrying a time-series panel tracking the request rate (rate(flask_http_request_total[1m])).

Verification & End-State Checks
To verify that the entire stack meets all specifications, run the following inspection commands:

1. Check Container Health & Status
Bash
docker inspect model-api prod-redis prod-nginx prod-prometheus prod-grafana prod-traffic --format='{{.Name}}: {{.State.Running}}'
Expected Result: All containers report true.

2. Verify Flask Metrics Endpoint
Bash
curl -s http://localhost:5000/metrics | head -n 15
Expected Result: Returns standard Prometheus exposition format (HTTP 200).

3. Test Proxy & Model Prediction
Bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:8085/predict \
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \
  -d '{"amount": 150.0, "hour": 14, "num_tx_past_day": 2}'
Expected Result: Returns JSON response containing {"is_fraud": 0} (or 1).

4. Verify Prometheus Targets
Bash
curl -s http://localhost:9090/api/v1/targets | grep -o '"health":"up"'
Expected Result: Confirms the model-api target job is healthy and up.

5. Verify Grafana Datasource & Dashboards
Bash
# List datasources
curl -u admin:grafana2026 http://localhost:3000/api/datasources

# Search user-created dashboards
curl -u admin:grafana2026 http://localhost:3000/api/search?type=dash-db
Expected Result: Lists the pre-provisioned Prometheus datasource and the custom "Fraud Detection API Request Rate" dashboard.
